{"id": "1.2-00025", "case_id": "github_secworks_sha256_direct_v1", "parent_id": null, "source": "github/secworks/sha256", "record_protocol": null, "panels": {"stored": "<h2>Agent task and scoring</h2><div class='card'><table><tbody><tr><th style='width:175px'>Input task</th><td class='path'>Project specification, interfaces, and permitted public assets</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Agent context</th><td class='path'>Direct agent workspace: <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00025&amp;file_q=public%2F'>public/</a> (5 files)</td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Expected output</th><td class='path'>Required RTL: src/rtl/sha256.v, src/rtl/sha256_core.v, src/rtl/sha256_k_constants.v, src/rtl/sha256_w_mem.v. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00025&amp;file=public%2Foutput_contract.json'>Open full output contract</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Hidden reference</th><td class='path'>4 RTL files under private/reference/; <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00025&amp;file=private%2Freference%2Fsrc%2Frtl%2Fsha256.v'>open first reference file</a></td></tr><tr><th style='width:175px'>Private score</th><td class='path'>Passed verification testpoints divided by total testpoints, using testbenches or formal verification<br><strong>2 verification testpoints:</strong> tb_sha256, tb_sha256_core. <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00025&amp;file=private%2Fscoring.json'>Open private scoring contract</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Case-stored agent instruction</h2><div class='card'><p class='muted'>These are case-stored instructions. A launcher may add framing or execution limits; its saved trajectory is the exact runtime record.</p><details><summary>public/instruction.md · <a href='#task=1.2&amp;case=1.2-00025&amp;file=public%2Finstruction.md'>Open raw</a></summary><pre># Implement the Secworks SHA-224/SHA-256 project\nRead specification/project.md (unchanged upstream README) and implement the four required RTL files with sha256 as the 32-bit bus top. Streaming/AXI wrappers are separate configurations. This core compresses already-padded 512-bit blocks; the caller performs padding. Both SHA-224 and SHA-256 are required.\n\nCurator interface clarification, reviewed against the pinned release before evaluation:\n- Rising-edge clock; asynchronous active-low reset_n. cs &amp;&amp; we writes a word-addressed register on an edge. cs &amp;&amp; !we selects a combinational read. No handshake/acknowledgment; unmapped reads and read_data when deselected/writing are zero. error is always zero in this release, including invalid addresses.\n- Identity registers 0x00/01/02 return 0x73686132/0x2d323536/0x312e3830. Control 0x08 bits 0/1 are one-cycle registered init/next strobes; bit 2 is mode (1 SHA-256, 0 SHA-224), reset to 1. Every control write also writes mode. Thus 0x05 starts a new SHA-256 message, 0x06 continues it; 0x01/02 select SHA-224. Read control reflects {mode,next,init}. Status 0x09 bits 0/1 reports ready/digest_valid through one wrapper register stage.\n- Block words 0x10..1f are read/write, MSW first. Digest words 0x20..27 are read-only, MSW first. Wrapper block/digest/control/status storage resets to zero except mode=1. Digest storage updates when core digest_valid is high. SHA-224 uses the first seven digest words; the eighth is the core&#x27;s extra chaining word, not part of the 224-bit digest.\n- init starts compression with the standard mode-dependent initial hash; next continues with the preceding chaining state. Keep mode fixed within a message. Wait for ready to become low then high before starting another block or reading the completed digest. Inputs are sampled at the accepted block command; wrapper block registers can be loaded while the previous block computes. Do not overlap init/next or issue commands while busy.\n- sha256_core implements the 64 standard rounds, 32-bit modular additions, message expansion and feed-forward. sha256_k_constants is the combinational 64-entry standard SHA-256 constant ROM indexed by round. sha256_w_mem loads the 16 MSW-first words on init and advances its sliding message schedule on next: W[t]=sigma1(W[t-2])+W[t-7]+sigma0(W[t-15])+W[t-16] modulo 2^32. Its w port supplies the selected initial word for rounds 0..15 and the expanded word afterward. sigma0=ROTR7 xor ROTR18 xor SHR3; sigma1=ROTR17 xor ROTR19 xor SHR10.\n- Core ready is high at reset; digest_valid clears on reset and a newly accepted command, and asserts on completion. Stable completed outputs and finite ready-handshake completion are required; internal state names are unrestricted.\n\nOriginal-document scope: README specifies the algorithm, two modes, unpadded block API, buffering and control-mode caveat. Exact bus addresses/identity/reset behavior above are curator clarifications from the pinned RTL. The original w-memory bench only prints state and is retained unscored; checked core and bus benches exercise its operation through the complete design.\n\nImplement every required path in output_contract.json. Declaration-only ports\nare in interfaces/modules.v. No initial/final, delays, simulation system tasks,\nforce/release or external includes are permitted. Only declared RTL is collected.\nMissing files, forbidden constructs or failed top compilation score zero. Each\nprivate point has equal weight and requires all of its checks to pass. Use\nterminal/file_editor, iverilog, vvp and Python iteratively; finish by saving RTL.\nRun `python3 checks/check.py` for public compilation feedback.\n</pre></details></div>"}, "files": [{"name": "case.json", "url": "assets/0c019ee177313e67716619d08e3a27d6ee19249fe4510d3c1c9d8931b24893b0.txt", "size": 10757, "binary": false, "section": "Other: case.json"}, {"name": "environment/runtime.json", "url": "assets/771e58998dfa59d19bd4591ce49673fcbadd56239829b74d2a9c5869aef77295.txt", "size": 4693, "binary": false, "section": "Other: environment"}, {"name": "private/original_reference/src/rtl/sha256.v", "url": "assets/091d2338614c2f14dd61f663fe172a912616c76ac4